# What South African Hiring Managers Want in a Product/UX Design Portfolio (2025/2026)

## TL;DR
- **Substance beats polish, and "process + measurable impact" is the local through-line.** South African design leaders and recruiters (OfferZen; Takealot's Calvin Pedzai; banks like Absa and Standard Bank) consistently say a portfolio must show 2–3 deep case studies that narrate the problem, the process/decisions, and a quantified outcome — tailored to the specific role. This largely mirrors global best practice, so the SA-specific value is in layering local nuance (banking/compliance context, remote-readiness signalling, currency/timezone positioning) rather than re-litigating fundamentals.
- **The SA market on "designer who builds with AI" is behind the US/EU discourse but catching up fast — and that is an opportunity.** SA AI-skill demand is surging (Pnet: +77% year-on-year, +352% since 2019), OfferZen data shows AI fluency + product thinking becoming baseline for engineers, but hard SA data specifically on *designers* using AI tools is essentially absent. A portfolio that credibly shows AI-accelerated workflows will stand out locally more than it would in San Francisco.
- **For banking/fintech roles (your background), lead with enterprise design-systems, compliance-aware and accessible design, and POPIA awareness.** SA financial-services hiring (Absa, Standard Bank, Nedbank, Discovery) runs rigorous, portfolio-heavy, multi-stage processes and expects you to defend design decisions "within a banking context" — trust, security, regulatory constraint, and scale.

## Key Findings

**1. What SA hiring managers and recruiters say they look for.** OfferZen — SA's largest tech talent marketplace — has reviewed hundreds of SA designer profiles and states plainly that "a strong portfolio of work has been the biggest indicator of how well someone will do on OfferZen," and that companies "value portfolios even more than a CV." Their guidance (drawing on Dropbox's Ruth Buchanan, InVision's Danny Roberts, and Glucode's Keagan Hoffman): pick your top 3 projects (one comprehensive, one constrained, one exploratory), show process over pure results, and "UX your portfolio for your employer" — tailoring it to the specific role. Calvin Pedzai, Head of Experience Design at Takealot, is the clearest local voice: he reviews portfolios as a recruiter and says an incomplete case study "introduces doubt in a recruiter's mind," and advises descriptive project headings, a clear problem/role/type/date summary, justification for each UX method used, visible iterations/wireframes, and evidence of testing and retesting.

**2. Job-market conditions shaping expectations.** SA's broader labour market is under strain — overall hiring was down 5% year-on-year in Pnet's August 2025 report (vacancy ads falling 7%), and youth unemployment reached 46.1% for ages 15–34 in Q1:2025 per Statistics South Africa (62.4% for 15–24). But design bucked the trend: the same Pnet report found "design, media, and arts vacancies rose 44% year-on-year, with hiring activity up 8%." Product Designer is a named top-demand skill in Pnet's Design, Media & Arts sector snapshot. The tech market has cooled and "returned to office" — OfferZen's 2025 report found "the share of fully remote developers has dropped below 40% for the first time since 2022, and the percentage of devs required to be in the office full time has jumped by nearly 30% over the past 12 months," and companies are hiring more selectively for senior/high-impact roles. Competition is real and portfolios are being used as a fast filter.

**3. Recruiter/platform and community guidance.** OfferZen is the dominant local platform and its portfolio guidance is the most authoritative SA-specific resource. Communities exist but are modest in scale: the Interaction Design Foundation local groups (Johannesburg ~245 members, Cape Town ~158, Pretoria ~96), a South African UX/UI WhatsApp community, IxDA Cape Town, UX South Africa (annual conference, Johannesburg, November), and Front-end Developers South Africa. These are primarily networking/learning venues rather than published portfolio-standards bodies.

**4. AI-fluency positioning.** The SA discourse for designers is still emerging. UX South Africa's own 2026 editorial flags that "the design toolkit is being rewritten in real time" (Figma AI, AI wireframing, code generation from specs) and that agencies globally are restructuring around these tools — framing SA as reacting to a globally-led shift. OfferZen's engineering data shows AI fluency is becoming a baseline expectation for engineers, but on-the-job AI-tool adoption in SA has actually lagged the hype ("Only 37.9% of developers are using coding assistants at work, and more than 40% of South African developers aren't using them at all," per OfferZen's 2025 report). Net: SA is behind the US/EU curve on formal AI expectations for designers, which makes credible AI-fluency a genuine differentiator locally right now.

**5. International-remote vs local positioning.** SA's timezone (SAST, UTC+2) overlaps almost fully with Western Europe/UK and gives 4–5 hours with the US East Coast — a widely-cited advantage that makes SA designers attractive to international remote employers. Guidance for remote applicants: explain SA experience in universal terms (e.g., "Takealot — South Africa's largest e-commerce platform"), quantify achievements, and pre-empt concerns by signalling remote-readiness (reliable fibre, power backup for load-shedding, timezone overlap). There is very little SA-specific, authoritative guidance for *designers* on tailoring portfolios differently for international vs local audiences — a genuine content gap.

**6. Banking/fintech specifics.** SA financial-services design hiring is rigorous and portfolio-centric. Job specs and interview breakdowns for Absa (Senior Product Designer, Johannesburg) and Standard Bank UX roles show a dedicated portfolio-review session (walk through 2–3 case studies, defend decisions and impact) plus possible take-home/live design challenges and a leadership culture-fit round. Standard Bank in particular has invested in "world-class research infrastructure (labs, devices, facilities)." Relevant local regulatory context to weave in: POPIA (SA's data-protection law, benchmarked to GDPR-equivalent standards for biometric/financial data), FICA/KYC/AML onboarding requirements, and WCAG accessibility.

## Details

### The core portfolio expectation is process + impact, tailored to the role
The single most authoritative SA-specific resource is OfferZen's long-running portfolio guide. OfferZen — which describes itself as "South Africa's largest tech talent marketplace" connecting job-seeking software, product and data professionals with over 2,000 companies — reviewed the profiles of hundreds of UI/UX designers and found "a strong portfolio of work has been the biggest indicator of how well someone will do on OfferZen." The most in-demand design candidates "often have over 14 interview requests." Their expert guidance boils down to:
- **Curate to ~3 projects.** "As someone who's evaluating other designers, I'm only going to look at probably three that show a little bit of breadth" (Ruth Buchanan, Dropbox). Pick one comprehensive, one constrained (time/resource-limited), one exploratory/personal.
- **Show the process, not just pretty screens.** "The single most powerful thing you can do to make sure your UI/UX portfolio gets noticed is to show your process."
- **Tailor it.** Keagan Hoffman (Glucode, a SA app studio): "Adapt your portfolio to the environment… Think of it as UXing your portfolio for your employer." And Danny Roberts (InVision): "Your portfolio should ALWAYS tell the viewer a concise story of the work presented and be tailored to the job you're applying for."
- **You have ~30 seconds.** "Amongst a mountain of other portfolios… yours has about 30 seconds to grab their attention."

Calvin Pedzai (Head of Experience Design, Takealot) reinforces this from a recruiter's chair. In published portfolio reviews (UX Planet, July 2024) he warns that an incomplete case study "introduces doubt in a recruiter's mind," and lists concrete criteria: descriptive project headings so recruiters can find relevant work; a clear summary of problem, designer role, project type and date; justification of each UX method and how it fed the next step; visible sketches/wireframes/sitemaps/user flows; and evidence of testing, feedback incorporation, and retesting. He also cautions that "most unsolicited redesigns and Dribbble shots lack technical feasibility and real-world constraints to be taken seriously" — a signal that SA hiring managers value realistic, constrained, shipped work over speculative concept art.

This is broadly consistent with global best practice, so for a follow-up to international research, the SA nuance is: (a) OfferZen is the channel where this gets applied at scale locally; (b) SA reviewers explicitly discount Dribbble-style concept work in favour of real, constrained projects; and (c) local case studies should be explained in universal terms for reviewers who may not know the SA company.

### Market conditions: design is a relative bright spot, but competition and RTO are real
The macro backdrop is soft. Pnet's August 2025 report found overall SA hiring "down 5% year-on-year, vacancy ads falling 7%," and Pnet has repeatedly emphasised a structural skills mismatch. Youth unemployment reached 46.1% for ages 15–34 in Q1:2025, per Statistics South Africa — a figure the agency notes "had climbed to 46,1%—a 9,2 percentage point increase" since 2015, with 15–24 at 62.4%. Against that, design is comparatively healthy: the same Pnet report noted "design, media, and arts vacancies rose 44% year-on-year, with hiring activity up 8%," and Product Designer appears as a top in-demand skill in Pnet's Design, Media & Arts sector snapshot, alongside Technical Writer and Multimedia Designer, with quoted monthly CTC salary bands in the low-to-mid R20,000s to mid-R30,000s.

On remote/onsite dynamics, OfferZen's 2025 developer report (the best available local proxy) states "the share of fully remote developers has dropped below 40% for the first time since 2022, and the percentage of devs required to be in the office full time has jumped by nearly 30% over the past 12 months" — with Johannesburg (the banking/enterprise hub) more office-oriented than Cape Town (more startups/product companies). Retrenchments touched nearly a quarter of developers' companies. The practical implication for portfolios: hiring is more selective and senior-weighted, so mid/senior candidates must show impact and judgement, not just craft.

Salary context (to calibrate seniority signalling): SA product-designer pay estimates vary widely by source — Payscale puts the average base around R372,000/year; Glassdoor's self-reported figure is lower; Levels.fyi shows senior/total-comp ranges into the high hundreds of thousands to ~R1m in Cape Town, especially at internationally-backed employers (e.g., a Cape Town Zen Educate Product Designer role advertised roughly R700k–R1m).

### The AI-fluency question: SA is behind the global curve, which is your opening
There is a clear asymmetry in the evidence. SA has strong *general* AI-skill demand data. Per Pnet's August 2025 report (via News24, 12 September 2025), "demand for AI-proficient professionals [is] skyrocketing by 352% since 2019" with "year-on-year growth of 77%" (H1 2024 vs H1 2025); AI-skilled roles applying tools in traditional fields grew 488% over six years. Pnet's Head of Data, Anja Bates, noted "Gauteng dominates AI professional demand, accounting for 58% of advertised roles, followed by the Western Cape with 24%" (KwaZulu-Natal 4%).

OfferZen's engineering data reinforces the trend: AI fluency and product thinking are increasingly treated as baseline expectations for engineers, with survey respondents noting "AI, product thinking, and system design are becoming core." But actual on-the-job tool adoption lagged — in OfferZen's 2025 report, "Only 37.9% of developers are using coding assistants at work, and more than 40% of South African developers aren't using them at all" (GitHub Copilot dominated, used by over 70% of assistant users; fewer than 1% used tools like Cursor and v0.dev).

But there is almost no SA-specific *designer* AI data. The best local, designer-focused commentary is UX South Africa's 2026 editorial arguing "the design toolkit is being rewritten in real time" (Figma's AI features, AI wireframing, image synthesis, code generation from design specs) and that SA design teams must decide what they uniquely contribute (strategy, systems thinking, customer empathy, cultural context) as AI absorbs production work. This is opinion/agenda-setting, not survey data — a gap worth stating plainly.

Interpretation: because SA's formal AI expectations for designers trail the US/EU, a portfolio that credibly demonstrates AI-accelerated workflows — using AI for research synthesis, rapid prototyping (e.g., prompt-to-prototype), design-system tooling, or shipping AI-powered product features — is currently a stronger differentiator in SA than in more saturated markets. Increasingly, SA and SA-facing job specs already ask for it (e.g., "experience using AI creative tools to accelerate concept development"; roles seeking curiosity about AI tools like Claude/AI prototyping). Position AI as a force-multiplier on your judgement, not a replacement for it — echoing OfferZen's framing that the durable skills are "the ones AI can't reliably produce: product taste, architectural judgment, and critical thinking."

### International remote vs local: same craft bar, different framing
The timezone advantage is the recurring SA selling point: SAST (UTC+2) overlaps almost entirely with UK/CET and gives 4–5 hours with US East Coast, and international companies specifically seek SA talent for this plus strong English and salary arbitrage. For an internationally-targeted portfolio/application:
- Translate local context ("Absa — one of SA's 'Big Four' banks"; "Takealot — SA's largest e-commerce platform").
- Quantify outcomes and lead with business metrics (conversion, retention, time-to-value).
- Signal remote-readiness explicitly (fibre, power backup for load-shedding, timezone overlap, async written communication).
- Note that SA residents remain taxable on worldwide income (SARS provisional-taxpayer registration) — relevant for structuring international remote work but not a portfolio matter.

For local roles (especially banking/enterprise), expect more emphasis on stakeholder management, working within regulatory and legacy-system constraints, and hybrid/onsite presence. There is a genuine shortage of published SA-specific guidance on *how designers should tailor portfolios differently* for these two audiences — most existing remote-work guidance is developer-centric or generic.

### Banking/fintech: what SA financial-services hiring wants to see
Given your Absa/Old Mutual background, this is where SA-specific signalling matters most. Evidence from local job specs and interview breakdowns:
- **Rigorous, portfolio-heavy process.** Absa's Senior Product Designer (Johannesburg) and Standard Bank UX processes both include a dedicated portfolio-review session — walk through 2–3 case studies, explain your role, decisions and impact — plus a technical/skills round (often a practical design exercise or take-home) and a leadership/culture-fit round. Expect to "be challenged on your design decisions and your understanding of user needs within a banking context."
- **Enterprise design-system experience is prized.** SA banking roles emphasise design systems, journey mapping, and end-to-end experience design across web and mobile at scale; Standard Bank has invested in dedicated research labs and infrastructure.
- **Compliance-aware, trust-first design.** Weave in awareness of POPIA (SA's Protection of Personal Information Act — treated as GDPR-equivalent for biometric/financial data), FICA/KYC/AML onboarding constraints, and consent/data-minimisation. Show you can make regulatory requirements "feel natural rather than burdensome."
- **Accessibility.** WCAG conformance and inclusive design (relevant to SA's linguistic diversity, mobile-first and lower-bandwidth users) are differentiators.
- **Local infrastructure realities.** Offline-friendly, load-shedding-resilient, low-bandwidth-aware design decisions resonate with SA financial-services and fintech reviewers.

Named SA banking design leaders confirm design is treated strategically. Nedbank's design organisation includes Terry Behan (Executive Head of Design), Kyley Potgieter (Head of Design Experience), and Ramo Phalatse (Head of Design Strategy). Phalatse has said (per TechFinancials/htxt, November 2020): "Design at Nedbank is embraced as strategic capability and engages with all aspects of the value chain… As part of the digital team we get to shape the future of the bank and the services we deploy to our clients." Nedbank Product Design Leads have also run internal AI-tooling workshops — a signal that even conservative SA banks now have AI on the design agenda.

## Recommendations

**Stage 1 — Rebuild around 3 role-tailored case studies (do first).** Curate to exactly three: one comprehensive end-to-end banking/enterprise project, one constrained/shipped project, and one exploratory piece (ideally AI-augmented). For each, lead with a scannable summary (problem, your role, project type, date, timeline) and structure as problem → process/decisions → quantified outcome. Explicitly show iterations, testing, and retesting — this is what SA reviewers like Pedzai check for. Benchmark to change: if you're getting portfolio views but no interviews, your case studies are likely too shallow or too "concept-art"; add constraints, decisions and metrics.

**Stage 2 — Add explicit business-impact and compliance framing for banking roles.** Quantify outcomes in business terms (conversion, task completion, support-call reduction, adoption). Add a short, tasteful demonstration of compliance-aware design: how you handled consent/POPIA, KYC/onboarding friction, accessibility (WCAG), and design-system governance at scale. Threshold: for Senior/Lead financial-services roles, at least one case study should visibly operate under regulatory/enterprise constraints.

**Stage 3 — Build a credible AI-fluency narrative now, while it's still a differentiator.** Add one case study (or a clearly-labelled section) showing how you use AI in your real workflow — research synthesis, prompt-to-prototype, design-system automation, or shipping AI-powered features — framed as amplifying your judgement. Benchmark to change: if SA job specs you're targeting start *requiring* (not just "advantaging") AI tool experience across the board, escalate this from a differentiator to a core, front-and-centre theme.

**Stage 4 — Maintain two positioning layers (local vs international).** Keep a single portfolio but prepare two framings: for international remote roles, translate SA context, quantify aggressively, and add a one-line remote-readiness signal (timezone overlap, connectivity, power backup); for local banking/enterprise roles, foreground stakeholder management, regulatory constraint, and hybrid availability. Use OfferZen as your primary local distribution channel and IxDF/UX South Africa/LinkedIn SA for network-building.

**Stage 5 — Prepare for a portfolio-defence interview, not just a portfolio.** SA banking processes will make you walk through 2–3 case studies live and defend decisions "in a banking context," and may include a take-home/live challenge. Rehearse a tight, inverted-pyramid narration (most important point first) for each project.

## Caveats
- **Designer-specific SA data is thin; much local evidence is developer-focused.** OfferZen's flagship "State of the Developer Nation" data covers software engineers, not designers, and skews Cape Town/Johannesburg, large employers, and male respondents. I have used it as a directional proxy for market conditions and AI trends and flagged it as such; do not read developer figures as designer figures.
- **OfferZen's dedicated portfolio guide is authoritative but dated (originally 2018).** Its principles (process, curation, tailoring) remain current and are echoed by newer SA voices (e.g., Pedzai, 2024), but it predates the AI shift.
- **No SA survey quantifies AI-tool expectations for designers specifically.** The "SA is behind US/EU" conclusion is an inference from adjacent data (developer AI adoption, Pnet AI-demand, UX South Africa commentary) plus contrast with global Figma surveys that do not separately report Africa. Treat it as a well-supported judgement, not a measured fact.
- **Salary figures vary dramatically by source and methodology** (Payscale vs Glassdoor vs Levels.fyi vs Indeed); ranges cited are indicative, not definitive.
- **Almost no authoritative SA-specific guidance exists on tailoring designer portfolios for international vs local audiences** — this is a real gap, and the recommendations here are extrapolated from remote-work and general portfolio guidance.
- **Some job-spec "interview process" details come from third-party job-aggregator "enhancement notes"** (e.g., designproject.io) that infer typical processes rather than quoting the employer directly; treat process specifics as representative rather than official.
- **Named Nedbank design-leader titles and the Phalatse quote date to 2020** (still the most recent public statements found); treat organisational specifics as directionally current rather than confirmed for 2026.